			"value":[{
				"name":"the result",
				"type":"",
				"description":"The <libURLftpCommand> <function> <return|returns> a <string> with the\nresult of the FTP command (if the <server> sends back a result) or an\nerror message. An <FTP> <server> always begins its response with a\nthree-digit code, followed by any other relevant information."
			}],
			"description":"Use the <libURLftpCommand> <function> to communicate with an <FTP>\n<server> and perform tasks that are not implemented in the \n<Internet library>. \n\nThe ability to send any FTP command to an FTP server is a powerful\nfunction that can enable you to provide full support for all FTP\nfunctionality in your application. To use it effectively, you must be\nfamiliar with FTP commands and responses and be able to properly\nconstruct an FTP command line.\n\n>*Tip:*  To get general information about an <FTP> <server>, issue the\n> FTP \"HELP\" command:\n\n    answer libURLftpCommand(\"HELP\",ftp.example.net)\n\n\nFor technical information about FTP server commands, see [RFC 959,\nsection 4.1.3](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc959).\n\n>*Important:* The <libURLftpCommand> <function> is part of the \n> <Internet library>. To ensure that the <function> works in a \n> <standalone application>, you must include this \n> <OpenXTalk custom library|custom library> when you create your \n> <standalone application|standalone>. In the Inclusions pane of the \n> <Standalone Application Settings> window, make sure the \"Internet\" \n> script library is selected.",
