			"description":"Use the <stdout> <keyword> to write data to the \n<standard output (glossary)>, usually the screen or console window.\n\nOn Unix systems, output from a program is normally written to the\nscreen, but can be redirected to go to another device, process, or file.\nThis redirection is done on the command line when the program is started\nup. \n\nOn a server in CGI mode, writing to <stdout> for the first time triggers\nany headers to be generated.\n\nWhen you use the <write to file> <command> with the <stdout> <keyword>,\nOpenXTalk writes data to the <standard output>.\n\n>*Note:* On <OS X|OS X systems>, the <standard output> is the Console\n> window, not the Terminal window.\n\nSupport for using <stdout> on Server was added in version 4.6.3.",
